---
import Image from "astro/components/Image.astro";
import Container from "@components/Container.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/Layout.astro";
import ImagePopout from "@components/ImagePopout.astro";
import StaticGraphic from "@components/StaticGraphic.astro";
import MultiCol from "@components/MultiCol.astro";
import Gap from "@components/Gap.astro";
import Nav from "@components/Nav.astro"
import Footer from "@components/Footer.astro"

import Thumb from './thumbnail.png';
import Gallery from '@assets/images/galleryText.png';
import GalBG1 from "@assets/images/gallery1.png"
import GalBG2 from "@assets/images/gallery2.png"
import Banner from './banner.png';
import Icon from './icon.png';
import Brb from './brb.png';
import Logo from './logo.png';
import Poster from './poster.png';
import SF6 from './sf6-top8.png';
import T8 from './t8-top8.png';
import Stream from './stream.png';
import StreamScreen from './stream-screen.png';
import TSH from './tsh-example.png';
import mhmI from './mhm-icon.png';
import mhmL from './mhm-long.png';
import mhmD from './mhmD.png';
import Station from './station.png';
import CheckIn from './checkIn.png';
import p1 from './photo1.png';
import p2 from './photo2.png';
import p3 from './photo3.png';
import p4 from './photo4.png';
import p5 from './photo5.png';
import p6 from './photo6.png';
import SkipLink from "@components/SkipLink.astro";

---

<Layout title= "Event Design">
  <Nav />
  <div id="gallery">
    <ImagePopout 
      image={Gallery} 
      alt="Opener for the image gallery" 
      w="300px" 
      galleryID="1"
    />
    <StaticGraphic 
      i1={GalBG1} 
      i2={GalBG2} 
      b="70px" 
      r="80px" 
      w="180px"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="content-contain">
    <ImagePopout image={Thumb} alt="Event Design Thumbnail" w="100%" galleryID="2"/>
    <div class="textgroup">
      <h1 class="header">Event Design</h1>
      <p>
        This breakdown will cover what it is like to design for an Esports event from top to bottom.
        There are a lot of unique aspects that go into designing for an Esports event that you wouldn't normally consider.
        Outside of regular advertising you need to account for things like where will the attendees register?
        How do you design for a streamed event? How do you convey information to viewers who tune it at a random point in time?
        All of these questions and more will be shown through the process of designing for the fundraiser event run by the organization Mid-Hudson Mayhem.
      </p>
    </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Event Branding</h2>
        <p>
          The first thing to do when designing for any event in general is decide on a name and brand style, the same of course applies here.
          The important thing with Esports is that it needs to be immediately recognizable to the community it is being marketed towards,
          there are so many different types of game genres and sub-genres that you could be marketing towards that it's important to be specific and obvious in marketing.
          It's also important to consider the intent of an event, branding will look very different if you're marketing for a competitive tournament as opposed to a relaxing convention.
          An important trend followed by most Esports events is to focus the branding on the community. Traditional sports tend to trend more sophisticated in their design styles
          but with Esports the community is much more grassroots and thus most events run a heavy focus on promoting to the little guy in their marketing rather than a multi-million dollar team.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          Let's talk about the project I worked on for this case study. Mid-Hudson Mayhem is an organization that manages and runs Fighting Game Tournaments,
          they have their own branding style and logo as you can see displayed. The event that I am designing for them is also a collaboration.
          Mid-Hudson Mayhem (MHM) just recently lost access to the venue that they used to hold events in and are fundraising in order to be able to find a new venue to run out of.
          That’s why they are collaborating with New Paltz Esports in order to use their venue space to jointly fundraise with them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={mhmI} alt="Mid Hudson Mayhem's icon logo" w="300px" galleryID="3"/>
        <ImagePopout image={mhmL} alt="Mid Hudson Mayhem's wordmark logo" w="500px" galleryID="3"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          With that context we can discuss what we talked about earlier, we are marketing to MHM members and people interested in Fighting Games more broadly with the intent to Fundraise being the primary objective of the event.
          Using that context I decided on the name “First to Fund”. This pulls from a fighting game adage where when you play a match it's “First to 2 or 3” with one player needing that amount of wins to win the match.
          As a bonus the title also evokes a call to action. You’ll notice that a lot of smaller community-focused events are either named after the venue they’re hosted in or include a sort of reference in their title,
          all to appeal to the average player and make them feel included.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          For the event's logo I combined the signature colors of both fundraising organizations, as well as applying those colors across the rest of the design style.
          I used the fist as it is a common and easy to understand symbol among the Fighting Game Community. Small allusions like this make for easy to understand and distinguish from other Esports designs.
        </p> 
      </div>
      <ImagePopout image={Logo} alt="Logo for the First to Fund event" w="500px" galleryID="1"/>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Advertisement</h2>
        <p>
          Now to move on to the more unique aspects of designing for an Esports event I like to break it down into 3 phases,
          the advertisement, on-site graphics, and post-event graphics. For advertisement of course you have to make the standard advertisement social post which you can see here,
          but uniquely there also needs to be an event page for participants to pre-register for an event, and those need to be designed with the necessary information listed on them.
          For this event we used a common tournament registration platform called start.gg. The page was made in order to convey all the necessary information to the attendees, that includes schedule, entry fees,
          donation information, game rules, location and communication. You can see the link to the page I designed for the event here: <a href="https://www.start.gg/npmhm">start.gg/npmhm.</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          The event page also comes with its own few designs, you can see below the icon and banner that are displayed in the website’s search engine.
          These are made to be easily readable and increase its shareability.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={Icon} alt="Tournament site Icon" w="300px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={Banner} alt="Tournament site Banner" w="500px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>On Site</h2>
        <p>
          The next part of event design is “on-site” graphics. With this section we mainly focus on the competitor and viewer experience of the event.
          For the competitor I made station numbers to help people with finding where they need to go and made QR Code flyers for each of the donation desks in order to make the barrier to entry for donations easier.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={mhmD} alt="Donation flyer for Mid Hudson Mayhem" w="200px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={CheckIn} alt="Check in desk flyer" w="300px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={Station} alt="Station marker for event layout" w="300px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          For the viewer side of things and something pretty unique to Esports I made a “stream layout”. A stream layout conveys all of the necessary information of a game or match to the viewer no matter
          what time they decide to start tuning in. This is incredibly important because without it a viewer would get lost as there is no in-game indication of match count or the players playing and it would make it
          impossible to follow along. The most important information to convey that I have highlighted here is Player Name, Player Sponsor, Game Count for each player and Position in the bracket.
          You can see below the example:
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={Stream} alt="Stream layout to be overlayed on stream" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={StreamScreen} alt="In-use example of the stream layout" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          This is all done through a program widely used in the FGC called “Tournament Stream Helper” which can translate information from the event page directly to OBS for the stream through the use of
          Javascript and styled through HTML and CSS coding which I have customized to fit the event’s branding.
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={TSH} alt="Behind the scenes for the stream layout setup" w="800px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <p>
          Aside from this I also added a “Be Right Back” scene that allows us to transition between games more smoothly.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ImagePopout image={Brb} alt="Be right back screen for the stream" w="700px" galleryID="1"/>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Post-Event</h2>
        <p>
          The last section of designing for an Esports Event is the “post-event” designs. These are very dependent on the type of event you’re running and can range from a Champion Graphic to a “Thanks for Coming” Graphic to
          nothing at all! For this specific event I had to make a “Top 8 Graphic” highlighting the Top 8 players of each tournament ran at the event, you can see what these look like below:
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={SF6} alt="Top 8 graphic for Street Fighter 6" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={T8} alt="Top 8 graphic for Tekken 8" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
      <div class="textgroup">
        <h2>Outcome</h2>
        <p>
          The event ended up being a big sucess! We got 42 attendees total and a few more that just showed up to check out the event. It also had about 80 live viewers on the Twitch stream. This was in big part due to following through on all the guidelines I had talked about.
          I kept my specific audience in mind and played to their strengths, made sure to emphasize the community and grassroots nature in the designs. I made sure to account for viewers at home by designing for streaming
          so that they could get access to the same information the players had. And for attendees of the event I used station numbers, donation posters, and the general room layout to make the experience fun and smooth.
          One important thing to note when I was talking with one of my collaborators was that they had noted that they've never actually finished an event by the time they were supposed to.
          For this event we had a time limit for how long we could use the room on top of that so it was crucial to finish on time. Through the use of the strong on-site design mentioned earlier we were actually able
          to complete the event just on time for an organization that has never been on time before. The application of event design in Esports is truly unique through all of these factors as events like this
          both take a long time to run and have difficulty conveying to wide audiences which is why design is ever so crucial here. You can see below an album of a few photos I took to document all of these designs in action!
        </p>
      </div>
      <MultiCol>
        <ImagePopout image={p1} alt="Photo from the event #1" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={p2} alt="Photo from the event #2" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={p3} alt="Photo from the event #3" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={p4} alt="Photo from the event #4" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={p5} alt="Photo from the event #5" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
        <ImagePopout image={p6} alt="Photo from the event #6" w="400px" galleryID="1"/>
      </MultiCol>
  </div>
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<script>
</script>

<style>
  .page-contain{
    display: flex;
    align-items: top;
    /* grid-template-columns: 650px 1fr; */
    justify-content: center;
    margin: var(--size-page-margin) 0;
  }
  .content-contain{
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    margin: 0 auto;
    gap: 3rem;
    background-color: var(--color-background);
    height: 100%;
    padding: 3rem;
    padding-top: 8rem;
    max-width: 1200px;
    clip-path: polygon(50% 0, 50% 0, 50% 100%, 50% 100%);
    animation: Open 1s both;
  }
  .content-contain p { max-width: 800px; }

  @keyframes Open{
    0%    { clip-path: polygon(50% 0, 50% 0, 50% 100%, 50% 100%); }
    100%  { clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 100% 100%, 0 100%); }
  }

  .textgroup h2 { color: var(--color-text); font-family: var(--font-headings);}
  .gallery-contain{
    width: 800px;
    padding: 1rem;
    background-color: var(--color-background);
  }
  #gallery{
    position: fixed;
    padding: 1rem;
    right: 40px;
    bottom: 0px;
    z-index: 1000;
  }
  .header{
    font-size: 48pt;
    font-weight: 800;
  }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    #gallery{
      transform: scale(60%) translateX(120px) translateY(100px);
    }
    p{
      font-size: 12pt;
    }
    .content-contain{
      padding: 1rem;
    }
  }
</style>